# XSH log1p(), log1pf() and log1pl()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Values are tested only where the page fixes them (special values, signed
# zeros, infinities, NaN propagation, the stated errors). The accuracy of
# results is not tested. Arguments pass through volatile objects so that
# nothing is folded at compile time. Error reports follow math_errhandling:
# errno is tested only when (math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO) is non-zero and
# the exception flags only when (math_errhandling & MATH_ERREXCEPT) is
# non-zero; a test of an error report is NOTINUSE if neither is. Rules shaded
# as IEC 60559 floating point (MX) are NOTINUSE where __STDC_IEC_559__ is not
# defined. The three types (name, name with f, name with l) are each tested.

assertion  XSH.log1p.001
anchor     functions/log1p.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.23
class      required
says       log1p of ordinary arguments (log1p(0.5); log1p(-0.5); log1p(10.0))
           returns without setting errno (which keeps its earlier value, 0 or
           ERANGE) and without raising an invalid, divide-by-zero or overflow
           exception.
test       t/001-ordinary-no-error.c

assertion  XSH.log1p.002
anchor     functions/log1p.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.24
class      required
says       A quiet NaN argument gives a NaN result with no error (errno
           unchanged, no error exception).
test       t/002-nan.c

assertion  XSH.log1p.003
anchor     functions/log1p.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       log1p(+0), log1p(-0) and log1p(+Inf) return the argument (a zero
           keeps its sign).
test       t/003-zero-inf.c

assertion  XSH.log1p.004
anchor     functions/log1p.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/log1p.html#ERRORS
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.23
class      required
says       log1p(-1) returns -HUGE_VAL as a pole error: errno is ERANGE where
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERRNO, and the divide-by-zero
           exception is raised where it includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/004-pole.c

assertion  XSH.log1p.005
anchor     functions/log1p.html#ERRORS
anchor     functions/log1p.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       log1p() of the finite values -2, -1.5 and the negated type maximum
           is a domain error: errno is EDOM where math_errhandling includes
           MATH_ERRNO, and the invalid exception is raised where it includes
           MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/005-below-minus-one-domain.c

assertion  XSH.log1p.006
anchor     functions/log1p.html#ERRORS
anchor     functions/log1p.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       log1p(-Inf) is a domain error, reported as for any domain error.
test       t/006-minus-inf-domain.c

assertion  XSH.log1p.007
anchor     functions/log1p.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       log1p of a positive and of a negative subnormal value returns a
           value no greater in magnitude than the type minimum (the argument
           itself is such a value).
test       t/007-subnormal.c
